 this token boundary, preventing partial matches like \"nemesis scanner\" when the contract is \"nemesis scan\".\n\nAlternatively, I could split both strings and verify the example begins with all command tokens:\n\n```javascript\nconst commandTokens = contract.command.split(' ');\nconst wrongCommand = examples.filter((example) => {\n  const exampleTokens = example.trim().split(/\\s+/);\n  if (exampleTokens.length < commandTokens.length) return true;\n  for (let i = 0; i < commandTokens.length; i++) {\n    if (exampleTokens[i] !== commandTokens[i]) return true;\n  }\n  return false;\n});\n```\n\nThis approach ensures the example starts with the complete command before any additional arguments.\n\nActually, a regex is cleaner here. I can match either the command alone or the command followed by a space and other tokens:\n\n```javascript\nconst wrongCommand = examples.filter((example) => !new RegExp(`^${contract.command}(?:\\\\s|$)`).test(example.trim()));\n```\n\nFor the authority check, I need case-insensitive matching on the authority field and the kind field. The authority property should match \"legal\" or \"security\", while the kind should match patterns like \"legal-notice\" or \"security-notice\" using a case-insensitive regex.\n```"}}
